What are you talking about? Usenet has NNTP protocol, the "Web" has HTTP
(for example). The Web is mostly carrying HTML text. Usenet is carrying
plain text, or hex-encoded binaries. All those protocols are standards.
While Usenet carries mostly unstructured (i.e. non-standardized) plain
text, the "Web" carries data at least in a standardized format; not that
this matters, though, since in both cases there's no real operational
semantics defined for that data[*] - which are of course an issue of the
underlying protocols. [*](In case of HTML it's only presentation hints.)

The problem with Web clients for Usenet access is that they are re-using
a method designed for some sort of application inappropriately for some
completely different task. Or, said from a different perspective; the
Web (and it's protocols and extensions) is nowadays used for everything.
Though the original thesis was that Usenet would be replaced by Web
forums. Probably. In case the new generations get to learn only the
contemporary inventions all ancient pearls will get forgotten and lost.

